From ec897ab8b937d963bb140e5e2096ea48829c86e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:04:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(init): drop LAN connections that leaked while rules were down

A restart (any save in LuCI, a WAN reconnect) takes the firewall rules
down in stop_service and puts them back at the end of start_service.
A LAN TCP connection opened in between goes straight to WAN through
masquerade, and conntrack keeps that NAT for the life of the
connection: the redirect added afterwards never applies to it. Browsers
and TV apps keep such HTTP/2 connections open, so blocked sites stay
unreachable for minutes after the restart.

Once the rules are back, delete the masqueraded TCP entries. The next
packet of a leaked connection is tracked anew and hits the redirect:
it is reset and the client reconnects through sing-box. A connection
that is meant to go direct is picked up mid-stream (tcp_loose) and goes
on through masquerade. Redirected connections (DNAT) and the router's
own connections (not translated) are not matched by --src-nat.

Checked in network namespaces with conntrack-tools 1.4.8: a connection
opened before the redirect kept reaching the server, after the delete
it was reset and the reconnect went to the redirect port; a direct, a
redirected and a router-originated connection carried on.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_011cyCFAA9hPeqN8wsgkxbrJ
---
 root/etc/init.d/homeproxy | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/root/etc/init.d/homeproxy b/root/etc/init.d/homeproxy
index 3167775..a89a49f 100755
--- a/root/etc/init.d/homeproxy
+++ b/root/etc/init.d/homeproxy
@@ -250,6 +250,19 @@ start_service() {
 	[ "$outbound_node" = "nil" ] || utpl -S "$HP_DIR/scripts/firewall_post.ut" > "$RUN_DIR/fw4_post.nft"
 	fw4 reload >"/dev/null" 2>&1
 
+	# A restart takes the rules down (stop_service) and puts them back above.
+	# A LAN connection opened in between went straight to WAN, and conntrack
+	# keeps its masquerade for as long as it lives: the redirect never applies
+	# to it, and to a blocked site it hangs for minutes. Drop the masqueraded
+	# TCP entries. The next packet of such a connection is tracked anew and
+	# meets the rules: a proxied one is reset (sing-box has no state for it)
+	# and the client reconnects, a direct one goes on through masquerade.
+	# Redirected connections are DNAT and the router's own ones are not
+	# translated, so neither is touched.
+	if [ "$outbound_node" != "nil" ] && command -v conntrack >"/dev/null"; then
+		conntrack -D -p tcp --src-nat >"/dev/null" 2>&1
+	fi
+
 	log "sing-box $(sing-box version -n) started."
 }
 
